# THL USB lockdown (pre-USBGuard layer)
#
# USBGuard handles the policy-level allow/deny. These udev rules provide
# a lower-level backstop: any USB device that connects while the screen is
# locked is immediately authorised=0 (blocked) before the kernel even
# tries to bind a driver.
#
# HOW IT WORKS
#   When a USB device connects, udev fires. We check whether the screen
#   is locked (swaylock is running) or the session is not active. If so,
#   we set authorized=0 which stops the device from enumerating.
#
# TO ALLOW A NEW DEVICE
#   Unlock the screen first, then plug in the device.
#   Or: echo 1 > /sys/bus/usb/devices/<bus>-<port>/authorized  (as root)

ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="usb", \
  RUN+="/usr/local/sbin/thl-usb-authorize %k"
